# --- build and cache ---------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/
.DS_Store

# --- maintainer notes, deliberately not published ----------------------------------
#
# These are on disk and in version control's *ignore* list, not in it. Two reasons, and
# the second is the one that matters:
#
#   1. they change for reasons that have nothing to do with the code;
#   2. the plan contains product strategy, competitive analysis and a monetisation
#      outline, and DESIGN.md refers to local machine paths. A public repository is the
#      wrong place for either.
#
# What *is* published is the technical architecture and what the project is: README.md
# and docs/ARCHITECTURE.md.
AGENTS.md
docs/PLAN.md
docs/DESIGN.md
docs/RELEASE_READINESS.md

# --- local agent configuration, also deliberately not published ----------------------
#
# The skills under .agents/skills/ tell the harness *how this checkout is worked on* --
# the pyjab dependency rules, the thread-affinity procedure, this machine's venv and
# proxy. They take effect from the working tree without being versioned. pyjab's own
# .gitignore does the same thing for the same reason.
#
# MANIFEST.in prunes the directory as well: an sdist is built from the working tree, so
# .gitignore does not protect it. Keep the two in step.
/.agents/

# --- harness state, if it is ever created inside the checkout ------------------------
#
# DSH keeps its own state under ~/.dsh; a .dsh/ here would be a checkout-local copy, and it
# holds session and workspace records. Ignored for the same reason as .agents/, and named in
# tools/check_local_only_files.py's LOCAL_ONLY so the guard enforces both mechanisms.
/.dsh/

# --- security material, which never belongs in a repository --------------------------
#
# Credentials come from the environment; a file that carries one is an accident waiting to
# be committed. These are excluded from the sdist too -- see MANIFEST.in -- and the guard's
# SECRET_PATTERNS records why the pattern list is not part of its own check.
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
credentials*
